# nidar_baseline.parm - the values this stack flew with BEFORE the tilt/accel
# tuning. This file is the revert: `param_profile:=baseline` (the launch
# default) pins these, so the vehicle behaves exactly as it did before
# nidar_tuning.parm existed.
#
# Every value here was read back from the live SITL on 2026-10-02 with
# PARAM_REQUEST_READ (see docs in RUNBOOK.md, "Param profiles"), and each one
# also equals the firmware's compiled-in default for this build
# (ArduPilot-4.6.0-beta1-8786-g4c98c9221a). Pinning them explicitly rather
# than relying on the compiled defaults means an upstream default change
# cannot silently move the baseline out from under an A/B comparison.
#
# WHY THESE FIVE
# --------------
# A multirotor accelerates horizontally by leaning: tan(theta) = a/g. The 2D    
# lidar's scan plane leans with it, so horizontal acceleration is what tilts
# the scan plane, and the three accel/jerk/angle params below are the only
# things upstream of that lean. Speed is in the list because it sets how much
# acceleration the controller is allowed to ask for in the first place.
#
# NAMES ARE BUILD-SPECIFIC. This build uses the SI-unit param names - WP_ACC
# (m/s^2), WP_SPD (m/s), PSC_NE_JERK (m/s^3). The older centimetre names
# (WPNAV_ACCEL, WPNAV_SPEED, PSC_JERK_XY) do NOT exist here, and an unknown
# name in a .parm file is read, found to match nothing, and silently ignored -
# no warning, no error, no effect. Verified against the source:
#   WP_      libraries/AC_WPNav/AC_WPNav.cpp        ("ACC", "ACC_CNR", "SPD")
#   ATC_     libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_AttitudeControl.cpp ("ANGLE_MAX")
#   PSC      libraries/AC_AttitudeControl/AC_PosControl.cpp      ("_NE_JERK")
# Prefixes come from ArduCopter/Parameters.cpp: GOBJECTPTR(wp_nav, "WP_"),
# GOBJECTVARPTR(attitude_control, "ATC_"), GOBJECTPTR(pos_control, "PSC").
# Note "PSC" has no trailing underscore, so the full name is PSC_NE_JERK.

# Horizontal acceleration limit, m/s^2. GUIDED velocity control reads this
# directly: ModeGuided::init sets pos_control's NE accel limit from
# wp_nav->get_wp_acceleration_mss() (ArduCopter/mode_guided.cpp:255). At 2.5
# that is atan(2.5/9.81) = 14.3 deg of steady-state lean.
WP_ACC          2.5

# Corner acceleration, m/s^2. 0 means "use 2 x WP_ACC" (AC_WPNav.h:144), i.e.
# 5.0 m/s^2 / 27 deg here.
#
# HONEST CAVEAT: in THIS stack that is latent, not active. WP_ACC_CNR is only
# read by AC_WPNav's S-curve waypoint tracker (AC_WPNav.cpp:421/463/497/616),
# which runs for AUTO missions and position targets. Nav2 drives this vehicle
# with VELOCITY commands through GUIDED, which never touches the S-curve path,
# so corner accel is not what spikes the lean at an L-turn - Nav2's own
# commanded velocity change is. It is pinned anyway because it costs nothing
# and it does bite the moment anyone flies a waypoint mission.
WP_ACC_CNR      0.0

# Maximum lean angle, deg, in every flight mode - the hard ceiling that no
# accel request can exceed. 30 deg permits 5.7 m/s^2.
ATC_ANGLE_MAX   30.0

# Horizontal speed, m/s. ModeGuided::init also takes pos_control's NE speed
# limit from wp_nav->get_default_speed_NE_ms(). 10 m/s is far above anything
# Nav2 commands in a 2.8 m maze corridor (it asks for 0.8), so in practice
# this is not the active limit - but it is the number ArduPilot will happily
# accelerate towards if Nav2's own cap is ever raised.
WP_SPD          10.0

# Horizontal jerk, m/s^3 - how fast the acceleration itself may change, i.e.
# how abruptly the lean is allowed to come on. 5.0 reaches 2.5 m/s^2 in 0.5 s.
PSC_NE_JERK     5.0

# Position-controller lean limit, deg. 0 = inherit ATC_ANGLE_MAX, which is
# what we want: one place to set the ceiling. Pinned at 0 in both profiles so
# a stale eeprom value cannot quietly override ATC_ANGLE_MAX for the position
# controller only.
PSC_ANGLE_MAX   0.0

# Attitude-controller input time constant, seconds. SITL's gazebo-iris.parm
# ships 0.20; Copter's own default is 0.15. Listed here so that switching back
# to this profile actually restores it - apply_param_profile.py reads the set
# of parameter names from THIS file, so anything the tuning profile writes and
# this one omits can never be reverted.
ATC_INPUT_TC    0.20

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR = 0  (NONE -> AutoYaw Mode::HOLD)
#
# REQUIRED. Nav2 runs MPPI with motion_model "Omni" and wz_max 0.0, i.e. it
# predicts every trajectory assuming the heading NEVER changes, and cmd_vel is
# published in base_link - the BODY frame.
#
# The SITL default is 2 (LOOK_AT_NEXT_WP_EXCEPT_RTL), which in GUIDED makes
# ArduPilot continuously yaw the airframe to face its own velocity vector
# (ArduCopter/autoyaw.cpp default_mode(), applied by mode_guided.cpp's velocity
# controller). The body frame then rotates underneath a command computed for a
# fixed heading, so the drone curves off the green /plan, Nav2 corrects, and
# ArduPilot yaws to follow the new velocity. That positive feedback loop is the
# violent weaving about the path.
#
# 0 holds heading, which is exactly what Nav2 already assumes. A 360 degree
# lidar loses nothing by never yawing, and it keeps scan matching clean.
WP_YAW_BEHAVIOR 0

# DDS_USE_NS = 0, the firmware default. Pinned explicitly so switching back
# to this profile actually reverts nidar_tuning.parm's DDS_USE_NS 1 - see
# that file's own comment for what this parameter does and why it matters.
DDS_USE_NS 0

# ARMING_CHECK = 0 — SIMULATION ONLY, never do this on a real airframe.
# Pinned here too so switching param_profile does not bring prearm-check
# friction back. See nidar_tuning.parm's own comment for the full reason.
ARMING_CHECK 0
